INT. CORPORATE PARKING GARAGE - LEVEL P4 - NIGHT

Concrete pillars striped in yellow. Fluorescent tubes flicker above rows of empty parking bays. A distant elevator dings. Somewhere, a drip.

SOLVEIG HARTMANN-OKORO, 40s, in a camel coat with a laptop bag over one shoulder, walks fast between the pillars, keys splayed between her knuckles. She looks like someone who has done this walk a hundred times and never liked it.

Her phone buzzes. She answers on the second ring, putting it to her ear.

                    SOLVEIG
Yes.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Are you at your car?

                    SOLVEIG
Nearly. Who is this?

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
A friend. Do you see the gray sedan?

She stops. Scans the garage. A gray sedan sits two rows ahead, engine off.

                    SOLVEIG
I see it.

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Don't go near it.

                    SOLVEIG
Why not?

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Because there's a man in the back seat.

Her breath catches. She does not move. Under the flicker of the lights she sees, very faintly, a shape in the window of the sedan.

                    SOLVEIG
(whispering)
How do you know?

                    VOICE (V.O.)
(over phone)
Because I'm standing behind you.

She spins. Nothing. Just a pillar and a puddle of oil, shining.

The phone drops from her ear. Behind her, the sedan's door clicks open.
